// A borrow of a FIELD is invalidated by an assignment to the WHOLE variable —
// the storage `i` names is overwritten either way, so `*i` afterwards reads
// what the assignment put there.
//
// This admitted (rc 0) until 2026-08-24 while the `&v` spelling of the same
// program refused, and the reason is the shape of the bookkeeping rather than
// a missing rule: `take_field_borrow` DID record the loan at path "f", and
// `field_borrow_conflicts` — the prefix query, with path "" meaning the whole
// value and colliding with every field path — already existed and was already
// called from four other sites. `case Code::Assign` simply read the two ROOT
// counters and never asked. One call closed it.
//
// The admit twin is `pass/bc_field_borrow_disjoint_assign` and it is what makes
// this fixture evidence: a rule that refused every field borrow would satisfy
// the line below and be wrong.
package bc_field_borrow_vs_whole_assign_fail;

struct Foo { f: u64, g: u64 }

fn main() -> i32 {
    let mut v: Foo = Foo { f: 22u64, g: 0u64 };
    let i: &u64 = &v.f;
    v = Foo { f: 23u64, g: 0u64 };
    return *i as i32;
}
